Z||000004||FTF Engineering||11-12-2001||09:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>The cave has been updated again...<P>Brakes... Thanks to Bruce at FTF Engineering, I now have the info about the solid faced rear pistons.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Ogre, You're quite welcome.<P>I took a look at the brake stuff in the cave and noticed a popular misconception:<P><B> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Rear Brake Adjustment<BR>The rear disk brakes require periodic use of the hand brake to maintain proper adjustment. I'm not going to try to explain why. It's just the way they are built.<P>If you are having problems with the rear brakes then always try cycling the brake handle three or four times before tearing them apart. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The rear brakes should self adjust every time you push the brake pedal. You don't need to use the parking brake.<P>When you push the brake pedal, you move the outer portion of the piston. If you move the outer portion and hold the lead screw (parking brake shaft inside the caliper) stationary, it will unload the internal clutch and the guts will spin inside the piston and "unscrew" themselves.<P>Then when you lift up your foot, the differential pressure across the clutch will go away and the clutch will re-engage which keeps the piston screwed out.<P>Really tough to explain without parts in the hand... They are pretty complicated little buggers....<P>Please let me know if there are any questions.<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||11-13-2001||12:28 AM||||Bruce... The brake articles are probably my biggest on going project. I think I understand what your saying. I agree that under normal circumstances that should be the case. In normal wear, at least for me, that seems to hold up. I'll have to figure out just how to revise that part.<P>You aren't kidding about hard to expalin.... You even have spares to take apart.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Try a patent search sometime. Took me a week just to figure out what keywords would turn useful results. Yuck. Gotta love those .gov servers... One of these days I'll have to drive up there and look at them. <P>Problem is it's a car part and what should happen ain't always going to. The worst occurances of the thing failing to auto adjust are right after brake work that required retracting the pistons. That just seems to be more than the caliper can easilly handle allot of the time.<P>That I can tell, my own calipers are in pretty good shape. (The original owner took pretty decent care of it.) The hand brake got the calipers closer but it wasn't until I did the manual adjustment that the brake performance and pedal travel returned to where they should be. So far I've intentionally left the brake handle alone but it probly hasn't been long enough to show if theres a problem or not... just did them last in the spring.<P>If you have ALLdata... The adjustment TSB is<BR>88Pontiac11<BR>bulletin # 88-5-10<BR>date: 6/88<BR>subject: Rear Caliper park brake service adjustment procedure (Could they make the name much longer?)<P>It applies to 82-88 Firebird, 84-87 Fiero, and 84-88 6000STE.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||MrPBody||11-13-2001||09:13 AM||||Ogre --<P>When you address clutch hydraulics, here's <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/015204.html" TARGET=_blank>another problem</A>.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||FTF Engineering||11-13-2001||09:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by theogre:<BR><B>Bruce... The brake articles are probably my biggest on going project. <P>I agree that under normal circumstances that should be the case. In normal wear, at least for me, that seems to hold up. I'll have to figure out just how to revise that part.<P>You aren't kidding about hard to expalin.... You even have spares to take apart.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ogre, Heh... I believe I've learned more from those rear pistons than from any other part I ever dissected! Only thing that rivaled them for complication packed into a small package was a hydraulic lifter.<P>I was so interested in the "solid faced" pistons that I went out and bought a set just to take a close look at them. Then after I found they weren't "solid"  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_wtf.gif"> as I thought they were, I destroyed one in the name of science (drilled a hole in the front to aid in the disassembly).<P>If you're ever in the area and want to see the cadaver, just let me know. I could also drop it in the mail if you would like. Probably would only be a couple bucks to ship to Delaware. All in the name of science.<P>Lust LMK.<P>Let's see... While we are on the subject... What else is interesting?<P>The piston guts are exactly the same for all pistons all years (and this includes the '88).<P>What else...? I've seen bearing failures on the adjuster mechanism. Either the bearing carrier cracks or one of the trust washers cracks from the load. That's why I remarked that the "solid face" pistons had a better bearing. I believe GM recognized the problem too and took the opportunity to include a better bearing.<P>I spent a bunch of time trying to find a source for the internal elastomeric seal. Unsuccessfully I might add!<P>I welded the face hole shut on a pair of '88 pistons that I was going to put on my car, but my pistons aren't leaking right now and I'm not gonna go looking for trouble.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I thought there was something else "interesting", but I'm drawing a blank. Probably just as well. I believe I'm officially rambling at this point!<P>Anyway, please let me know if there's anything I can do to help.<P>-Bruce at FTF Engineering||||reg||1
